WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 011, en. Timings estimated from the script.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.615
<v Gwen>Bore da! Worrall's Directory of South Wales, off the carrier's cart. Here, Llandough parish, Penarth section: 'Williams John, Great house.' In print for all Cardiff.

2
00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:18.812
<v the Bute estate agent>A directory prints whoever answers the canvasser, Gwen. It puts John Williams at Great House. It doesn't tell you who owns the place.

3
00:00:19.162 --> 00:00:28.008
<v Gwen>Slater's had him there in sixty-eight too, mind. And the land? The Marquess is lord of the manor, same as half this parish.

4
00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:46.435
<v the Bute estate agent>Lord of the manor, yes. Whose freehold this ground is, a directory can't tell you. And his lordship's business today is the limeworks. Thirty-three acres, formerly part of Great House Farm, for the Llandough Limeworks. The plan's drawn and engrossed on the deeds at the estate office.

5
00:00:46.785 --> 00:00:51.015
<v Gwen>Daniel Thomas and Son's kiln and quarry are down there already.

6
00:00:51.365 --> 00:01:07.519
<v the Bute estate agent>Which brings me to the poor rate. October, Llandough. John Williams, farm, owner Lord Bute, about seventy-six acres, gross forty-one pounds nought shillings. Lord Bute, woods in hand, thirty-two acres, three roods, thirty-nine perches. Daniel Thomas and Son, lime kiln and quarry.

7
00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:16.331
<v Gwen>So John's charged for seventy-six acres, and the limeworks gets thirty-three. Does your book make him a tenant? Yearly? On a lease?

8
00:01:16.681 --> 00:01:23.604
<v the Bute estate agent>It makes him the occupier the overseers charge. How he holds is the estate's affair, not the parish's.

9
00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:47.954
<v Nia>Worrall's 1875, page 324, digitised by the University of Leicester Special Collections: a Williams farmer at 'Great house', Penarth section. The Legal Wiki follows him on: Slater's 1868 and 1880, Owen's 1887 and 1891, the 1901 Cardiff Directory, all at Great House. A listing shows where he lived, not who held title.

10
00:01:48.304 --> 00:02:07.150
<v Nia>The National Library of Wales catalogue for Bute deeds D153/4 to 8 reads: 'land (33 a.), formerly part of Great House Farm, for the Llandough Limeworks (33 a.), 1876-1912.' Plan engrossed on D153/3, 1875, and 4, 1876. The deeds, their parties, the exact parcel: we haven't read them yet.

11
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:20.962
<v Nia>And the rate book: the same sequence of names appears in a rate of twentieth August, eighteen-seventy-something. The last figure can't be read. It shows who was charged, for what. It doesn't show a lease.
